WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.534 --> 00:00:02.734
-Welcome, welcome. Thank you
for coming on the show.

00:00:02.734 --> 00:00:05.200
I know you know
I'm a giant fan of yours.

00:00:05.200 --> 00:00:06.767
-Can I first say one thing?
-Yeah.

00:00:06.767 --> 00:00:08.734
-Dreams come true.
I mean, the last time

00:00:08.734 --> 00:00:11.868
I was in this studio,
I was back there.

00:00:11.868 --> 00:00:14.133
And to sit here is insane.

00:00:14.133 --> 00:00:18.334
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:18.334 --> 00:00:21.501
-I'm happy --
-Well, I mean,

00:00:21.501 --> 00:00:23.200
I'm not expecting you
to remember this,

00:00:23.200 --> 00:00:26.634
but I got a D.M.,
as one does on Instagram,

00:00:26.634 --> 00:00:28.834
and it said, "Oh, hey, Matt.
I'm a fan.

00:00:28.834 --> 00:00:30.934
Keep up the good work -- J."

00:00:30.934 --> 00:00:33.968
And it was from
some troll account, I thought,

00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:35.300
of course, right?
-Yeah.

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:37.868
-And I called my daughter in,
Clara, and I was like,

00:00:37.868 --> 00:00:41.234
"Clara, do you think
Jimmy Fallon really sent this?"

00:00:41.234 --> 00:00:43.067
And she's like,
"Dad, that is for real!

00:00:43.067 --> 00:00:46.133
That is so for real!"
And I was like, "No way!"

00:00:46.133 --> 00:00:48.567
And then, like, here we are.
It's crazy.

00:00:48.567 --> 00:00:50.501
Thank you.
-No, I'm a big fan of yours.

00:00:50.501 --> 00:00:52.300
Yeah, so I had to slide
into your DMs.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.100
-That didn't sound good.
[ Laughter ]

00:00:54.100 --> 00:00:57.200
-...and tell you -- tell you
what a great job you're --

00:00:57.200 --> 00:00:58.467
Here's -- I found --

00:00:58.467 --> 00:01:01.834
I don't know how I found
this -- this magazine,

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:04.934
but I also subscribe to --
You have a Substack thing.

00:01:04.934 --> 00:01:06.667
-Yeah.
-But I have --

00:01:06.667 --> 00:01:08.701
I love this
because it's everything.

00:01:08.701 --> 00:01:13.367
It's food, it's cars,
it's cool watches.

00:01:13.367 --> 00:01:17.167
It's stuff that, like --
like -- like, this guy...

00:01:17.167 --> 00:01:21.234
makes Saa-- he collects Saabs
or cleans up Saabs.

00:01:21.234 --> 00:01:22.601
-You could buy that car now.

00:01:22.601 --> 00:01:24.334
I think it's on Bring a Trailer.

00:01:24.334 --> 00:01:27.801
I mean, the magazine started
because I selfishly

00:01:27.801 --> 00:01:31.267
wanted to create
something for myself.

00:01:31.267 --> 00:01:32.901
-Really?
-Because I was in the --

00:01:32.901 --> 00:01:34.534
I kind of grew up
in the magazine business.

00:01:34.534 --> 00:01:36.300
I was a photographer,
and then I was working

00:01:36.300 --> 00:01:37.634
in Legacy Publishing,

00:01:37.634 --> 00:01:41.734
but I was always
buying 6 to 8 titles

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:45.434
to kind of, you know,
feed my magazine lust.

00:01:45.434 --> 00:01:47.267
-Yeah. Like, so you get
a food magazine,

00:01:47.267 --> 00:01:50.400
you get a "GQ,"
you get a "Bon Appetit."

00:01:50.400 --> 00:01:52.167
You get -- yeah.
-All of that.

00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:54.534
All those magazines
in one magazine.

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:56.000
Did you have a hard time
paying for this?

00:01:56.000 --> 00:01:58.133
Like, how...
-Well, I always wanted

00:01:58.133 --> 00:02:01.033
to do this magazine,
and, basically, my wife and I

00:02:01.033 --> 00:02:04.734
got fired from --
from Legacy Publishing.

00:02:04.734 --> 00:02:06.601
And I was like, "Oh, my God,
congratulations.

00:02:06.601 --> 00:02:08.200
Like, this is the best thing
that's ever happened.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.467
Now we could kind of do
exactly what we wanted."

00:02:10.467 --> 00:02:11.534
-I love this attitude.

00:02:11.534 --> 00:02:13.634
-So, my wife
is a creative director,

00:02:13.634 --> 00:02:15.100
has been a creative director.
And I was like,

00:02:15.100 --> 00:02:17.934
I knew we kind of
could build a magazine.

00:02:17.934 --> 00:02:20.467
-Yeah.
-But I didn't know how

00:02:20.467 --> 00:02:21.701
I was gonna pay for it.

00:02:21.701 --> 00:02:23.667
I didn't know how we were
going to distribute it.

00:02:23.667 --> 00:02:26.534
And one night --
I live in Brooklyn --

00:02:26.534 --> 00:02:29.200
a London planes tree,
which are those big trees

00:02:29.200 --> 00:02:31.434
that you see along the streets
of neighborhoods

00:02:31.434 --> 00:02:35.300
like mine in Park Slope,
broke off, fell, and landed

00:02:35.300 --> 00:02:40.200
on the only car on the block,
which was my car.

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.901
So it landed -- it landed down
the middle of this Land Rover.

00:02:42.901 --> 00:02:44.267
It completely
squashed the car.

00:02:44.267 --> 00:02:47.701
No other car was hit.
And then, the next day,

00:02:47.701 --> 00:02:52.067
I was sitting at my stoop
with the insurance adjuster,

00:02:52.067 --> 00:02:54.601
sitting on the stoop
with the insurance adjuster.

00:02:54.601 --> 00:02:57.334
The city came by
with a truck and a crane,

00:02:57.334 --> 00:02:58.734
and the insurance adjuster --

00:02:58.734 --> 00:03:00.701
You know, they want you
to keep the car, always, right?

00:03:00.701 --> 00:03:02.601
And he's like, "Oh, okay, yeah.

00:03:02.601 --> 00:03:05.367
I think we're gonna be able
to salvage this car, Matt."

00:03:05.367 --> 00:03:08.534
And the city came by
with one of those crane trucks,

00:03:08.534 --> 00:03:10.801
and it picked it up,
picked up the tree,

00:03:10.801 --> 00:03:15.234
and it slipped, and the tree
fell again onto the car.

00:03:15.234 --> 00:03:17.834
It was like Bugs Bunny.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:17.834 --> 00:03:19.801
-He goes, "Now we can't
fix the car, Matt."

00:03:19.801 --> 00:03:21.968
-And he looked --
he put -- he closed his,

00:03:21.968 --> 00:03:25.567
like, 1985 Dell computer.
[ Laughter ]

00:03:25.567 --> 00:03:28.634
And he was like, "We're gonna
cut you a check for the car."

00:03:28.634 --> 00:03:31.901
And I came in --
He cut me a check right there.

00:03:31.901 --> 00:03:33.167
I came into the house,
and I said,

00:03:33.167 --> 00:03:35.567
"We are gonna pay
for this magazine

00:03:35.567 --> 00:03:37.667
with that check
from the fallen car."

00:03:37.667 --> 00:03:38.968
-Wow.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:38.968 --> 00:03:40.834
And that's how it all started.
-That's how it happened.

00:03:40.834 --> 00:03:44.200
And now you have that.
And then, led to your books.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:46.334
"A Man &amp; His Kitchen."

00:03:46.334 --> 00:03:48.000
I have the other books --

00:03:48.000 --> 00:03:50.734
"A Man &amp; His Watch,"
"A Man &amp; His Car."

00:03:50.734 --> 00:03:53.601
-That's right.
-"A Man &amp; His Kitchen."

00:03:53.601 --> 00:03:55.968
All this stuff is like --
it's your taste.

00:03:55.968 --> 00:03:58.534
It's all in your brain,
the stuff you like to cook.

00:03:58.534 --> 00:03:59.834
There's the stuff
you like to do.

00:03:59.834 --> 00:04:02.767
I know that you lived in
an Airstream for like a year,

00:04:02.767 --> 00:04:05.033
I want to say,
and you grilled on a Weber grill

00:04:05.033 --> 00:04:07.934
for a whole year, every dinner.
-That was at William Brown Farm,

00:04:07.934 --> 00:04:10.400
and that was his farm that we --

00:04:10.400 --> 00:04:12.767
It's a piece of land
we bought in Upstate New York

00:04:12.767 --> 00:04:15.767
almost 20 years ago,
and we found this piece of land.

00:04:15.767 --> 00:04:17.133
There was a mobile home on it

00:04:17.133 --> 00:04:21.667
and a barn, like one of those
Fisher-Price red barns.

00:04:21.667 --> 00:04:24.133
And we got rid of
the mobile home --

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:25.634
key word being "mobile."

00:04:25.634 --> 00:04:29.667
And we lived in
an Airstream, seasonally,

00:04:29.667 --> 00:04:32.634
until, like, the hose froze.
And that --

00:04:32.634 --> 00:04:34.133
And then we built this house

00:04:34.133 --> 00:04:37.667
and all these kind of
great lifestyle experiments

00:04:37.667 --> 00:04:40.400
that happened
at William Brown Farm.

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:45.067
And this book is a collection
of those recipes,

00:04:45.067 --> 00:04:46.467
how we entertained,

00:04:46.467 --> 00:04:48.734
and how we share meals
with family and friends,

00:04:48.734 --> 00:04:51.367
and, you know, food souvenirs

00:04:51.367 --> 00:04:53.234
that I bring back
from traveling,

00:04:53.234 --> 00:04:54.868
things I love
from Upstate New York,

00:04:54.868 --> 00:04:57.133
like hot dogs and chicken wings.
-Yeah.

00:04:57.133 --> 00:04:58.701
The thing I love
about you is, like,

00:04:58.701 --> 00:05:00.334
there's -- there's
something in there.

00:05:00.334 --> 00:05:02.601
There's like a beautiful old
watch that you've got to go,

00:05:02.601 --> 00:05:04.200
"Oh, my God, it's a
gorgeous-looking watch,"

00:05:04.200 --> 00:05:05.834
or a great suit.
And then you're like,

00:05:05.834 --> 00:05:08.734
"And here's how
to boil hot dogs in beer."

00:05:08.734 --> 00:05:10.901
And you go -- yeah.
That makes it fun.

00:05:10.901 --> 00:05:13.634
-Yeah. That's one of
my favorite recipes in the book.

00:05:13.634 --> 00:05:15.300
-Were you always into cooking?

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:17.601
-You know, I was -- I grew up
in a big Italian family

00:05:17.601 --> 00:05:19.534
in Upstate New York,
in Binghamton. Shout-out.

00:05:19.534 --> 00:05:23.234
-Yeah, Binghamton. Absolutely.
I know Binghamton very well.

00:05:23.234 --> 00:05:26.634
-And my aunt had a diner,
Laura's Luncheonette.

00:05:26.634 --> 00:05:32.100
My uncle had a bar aptly named
Uncle Tony's that served food.

00:05:32.100 --> 00:05:35.834
So I kind of grew up
around these kind of amazing,

00:05:35.834 --> 00:05:37.801
mostly women who were
cooking and nurturing

00:05:37.801 --> 00:05:40.968
and, you know --
and I was hiding out, like,

00:05:40.968 --> 00:05:43.501
you know, trying not to get
abused by an uncle,

00:05:43.501 --> 00:05:45.100
you know -- not physically.

00:05:45.100 --> 00:05:46.868
-Thank you.
-I think -- hoo!

00:05:46.868 --> 00:05:49.701
-Verbal abuse.
And I, uh...

00:05:49.701 --> 00:05:51.934
[ Laughter ]

00:05:51.934 --> 00:05:56.300
-You can read all about it
in "A Man &amp; His Therapist."

00:05:56.300 --> 00:05:58.467
It's a great one.
-Tell me about your uncle.

00:05:58.467 --> 00:06:00.400
-Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:06:00.400 --> 00:06:03.467
-And so I grew up around
all that -- all that stuff.

00:06:03.467 --> 00:06:06.801
And when I was up at the farm,

00:06:06.801 --> 00:06:11.133
I started keeping a journal
about all these experiences.

00:06:11.133 --> 00:06:14.167
But, like, my early influences
with food

00:06:14.167 --> 00:06:16.434
was these diners
and these restaurants.

00:06:16.434 --> 00:06:19.634
And I loved cooking shows
on television.

00:06:19.634 --> 00:06:22.300
Like, television
was my educator.

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.667
-What was your favorite show?
-I loved Jacques Pépin.

00:06:24.667 --> 00:06:26.701
I loved The Galloping Gourmet.
-Love Jacques Pépin.

00:06:26.701 --> 00:06:29.300
-Yeah, I think you made
an omelet one time with Jacques.

00:06:29.300 --> 00:06:30.767
-Yeah. Oh, my God,
I'll never forget it.

00:06:30.767 --> 00:06:33.067
-And I loved
cooking shows so much,

00:06:33.067 --> 00:06:35.200
and I was living
in such a deep fantasy life

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:36.334
in Upstate New York,

00:06:36.334 --> 00:06:39.234
I used to pretend
I had a cooking show.

00:06:39.234 --> 00:06:44.868
And my mom had a beauty shop
in the house, as one does.

00:06:44.868 --> 00:06:47.067
And I remember,
I'd be in the kitchen,

00:06:47.067 --> 00:06:48.801
which was above the beauty shop,
and there was

00:06:48.801 --> 00:06:51.067
this picture window
looking out to the backyard,

00:06:51.067 --> 00:06:53.167
and I would be making
a bologna sandwich.

00:06:53.167 --> 00:06:56.167
So I'd say like, "So you take
two pieces of bologna,

00:06:56.167 --> 00:06:57.901
you put mayonnaise
on one side, a little --

00:06:57.901 --> 00:06:59.968
You crush the potato chip
into the --"

00:06:59.968 --> 00:07:02.868
And my mom would yell up
from the basement.

00:07:02.868 --> 00:07:07.267
She would say, "Matthew,
who are you talking to?"

00:07:07.267 --> 00:07:08.334
[ Laughter ]

00:07:08.334 --> 00:07:09.901
And I would just say, "Mom,

00:07:09.901 --> 00:07:12.000
I'm talking to
my television audience."

00:07:12.000 --> 00:07:13.634
And she would just say,
like, "Okay."

00:07:13.634 --> 00:07:15.133
Like, she never --
[ Laughter ]

00:07:15.133 --> 00:07:17.100
-See?
But look who you became?

00:07:17.100 --> 00:07:19.133
-She never, like --
I don't think she --

00:07:19.133 --> 00:07:20.734
I think she still thinks
I have that show.

00:07:20.734 --> 00:07:22.801
-Yeah. Congratulations.
Yeah.

00:07:22.801 --> 00:07:26.667
I love this pose that you do.
You post these on Instagram.

00:07:26.667 --> 00:07:28.267
You're just always posing
with a drink,

00:07:28.267 --> 00:07:30.701
a nice cocktail, and some,
like, raw meat or something.

00:07:30.701 --> 00:07:32.701
-Well, that's called
the meat pic.

00:07:32.701 --> 00:07:35.300
-Yeah.
-Be careful if you google that.

00:07:35.300 --> 00:07:37.300
You know?
[ Laughter ]

00:07:37.300 --> 00:07:38.868
You want to be really...
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:07:38.868 --> 00:07:41.133
Don't google that.
Yeah, yeah, no, don't google it.

00:07:41.133 --> 00:07:43.968
No, don't -- We'll think of
a different name for it, yeah.

00:07:43.968 --> 00:07:46.133
-But if you hashtag #MeatPic
on Instagram,

00:07:46.133 --> 00:07:48.934
it's a pretty --
it's a straight dive into these.

00:07:48.934 --> 00:07:50.701
[ Laughter ]
-...into these pictures.

00:07:50.701 --> 00:07:51.701
-We hope.
-We hope.

00:07:51.701 --> 00:07:53.467
-We hope, yeah.
We just hope.

00:07:53.467 --> 00:07:54.767
I was thinking,
if you don't mind,

00:07:54.767 --> 00:07:56.200
I'd like for maybe
you to teach me

00:07:56.200 --> 00:08:00.133
how to maybe
do the pose tonight with some.

00:08:00.133 --> 00:08:01.801
-Well, you need a tray of meat,

00:08:01.801 --> 00:08:03.901
which is really helpful.
-How did this come about?

00:08:03.901 --> 00:08:06.767
-So, we used to visit these
friends who had --

00:08:06.767 --> 00:08:08.100
I don't know --
I don't know --

00:08:08.100 --> 00:08:09.901
10 kids, 8 kids.

00:08:09.901 --> 00:08:13.334
And we would always be like
10 or 12 people for dinner.

00:08:13.334 --> 00:08:16.000
And there would always
be so much food.

00:08:16.000 --> 00:08:18.734
And I remember, one time,
there was this cookie sheet

00:08:18.734 --> 00:08:21.567
just, like, filled
with sausages and stuff,

00:08:21.567 --> 00:08:23.033
and I was like,
"That is so absurd.

00:08:23.033 --> 00:08:24.968
Take a picture with me
with this thing,"

00:08:24.968 --> 00:08:27.434
and I had my picture taken.

00:08:27.434 --> 00:08:29.901
And then every time
there was a big pile of meat,

00:08:29.901 --> 00:08:32.200
we just kept doing it,
and then we kept posting it.

00:08:32.200 --> 00:08:33.934
And then my wife
doesn't leave me alone.

00:08:33.934 --> 00:08:35.100
Like, if we're cooking,
she's like,

00:08:35.100 --> 00:08:36.567
"That is definitely a meat pic."

00:08:36.567 --> 00:08:41.067
And so we just started doing it.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:41.067 --> 00:08:42.434
And thank God.
-I love your wife.

00:08:42.434 --> 00:08:44.467
-Thank God for the Internet.
The --

00:08:44.467 --> 00:08:48.734
You know, people send me
their versions of the meat pic

00:08:48.734 --> 00:08:51.367
from all shapes and sizes.
[ Laughter ]

00:08:51.367 --> 00:08:53.367
Yeah.
-Yeah. Again, I'm so sorry.

00:08:53.367 --> 00:08:55.300
Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:08:55.300 --> 00:08:57.267
Censored.
-Yeah.

00:08:57.267 --> 00:09:00.934
-But, you know, I've had
meat pics from vegetarians...

00:09:00.934 --> 00:09:02.300
[ Laughter ]

00:09:02.300 --> 00:09:04.801
...pescatarians...
-Yeah, yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:09:04.801 --> 00:09:06.334
-...my uncle.
-Yeah. Oh, sure.

00:09:06.334 --> 00:09:07.501
Your uncle.
Yeah, of course.

00:09:07.501 --> 00:09:11.234
[ Laughter and applause ]

00:09:11.234 --> 00:09:13.400
[ Rimshot ]

00:09:13.400 --> 00:09:15.667
-I've always wanted a rimshot.
-You got to do it.

00:09:15.667 --> 00:09:17.367
Come on. You got a rimshot
on "The Tonight Show."

00:09:17.367 --> 00:09:19.400
I thought we could do a pic.
-You want to give it a try?

00:09:19.400 --> 00:09:21.501
-Yes. What do you have?
A beautiful cocktail right here.

00:09:21.501 --> 00:09:24.100
-So, my -- one of my
favorite drinks are the --

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:25.667
My favorite drink
is the Negroni.

00:09:25.667 --> 00:09:27.234
I wrote a book about Negronis.

00:09:27.234 --> 00:09:31.000
I have more pictures of Negronis
than my -- sorry, Clara --

00:09:31.000 --> 00:09:33.300
than my daughter.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:33.300 --> 00:09:35.200
But I'd like to do it...

00:09:35.200 --> 00:09:37.067
-Yeah, I saw Clara
is in the book, by the way.

00:09:37.067 --> 00:09:39.234
A couple of recipes for Clara,
the banana bread.

00:09:39.234 --> 00:09:41.467
Shout-out to Clara.
-Yeah, she runs a diner.

00:09:41.467 --> 00:09:42.934
-Yeah. All right.
Why don't we do this?

00:09:42.934 --> 00:09:44.400
Let's do this right here.
-Okay.

00:09:44.400 --> 00:09:46.167
-How do I...
-I'm right-handed,

00:09:46.167 --> 00:09:47.434
so I hold the drink.

00:09:47.434 --> 00:09:49.100
-All right. You hold the drink
in the right hand.

00:09:49.100 --> 00:09:51.901
-Right. And then, you know,
people do this wrong

00:09:51.901 --> 00:09:54.367
all the time.
Like, they'll hold it low

00:09:54.367 --> 00:09:56.334
or they'll get lazy
with the drinking.

00:09:56.334 --> 00:09:59.234
It's got to go high.
[ Laughter ]

00:09:59.234 --> 00:10:01.934
So you got to get it
above the shoulder.

00:10:01.934 --> 00:10:04.701
Right? And then you tip it
toward camera.

00:10:04.701 --> 00:10:07.367
And let's say cheers.

00:10:07.367 --> 00:10:08.367
Cheers.
-Cheers.

00:10:08.367 --> 00:10:09.434
You sip your drink.

00:10:09.434 --> 00:10:12.734
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:10:12.734 --> 00:10:13.767
Mmm!
-Mmm!

